# Lore.Land Story Boonhonk
# Working canon for WAPboy, Wonder About Pi(e), and the Boon/Bane/Bone constellation.
# This surface holds durable principles and flexible story seeds; it is not a locked plot.
#>lore_land_story_boonhonk
#:canon #!story-boonhonk #!reference

^"boonhonk"{
  .status: "working"
  purpose: "Give the multidimensional media ecosystem a shared story grammar without closing the story too early."
  .audience: ["reader", "returning reader", "maker", "spwashi.com witness"]
  surface_relation: {
    lore_land: "the adventure and its mythic consequences"
    spwashi_com: "the maker's background register: organization, incentives, structures, and lived perspective"
    zine: "portable fragments, diagrams, marginalia, and alternate entrances"
  }
}

^"central_question"{
  question: "Can a figure made to signify something become a person?"
  answer_shape: "WAPboy is not announced as the protagonist; the world keeps making room for him until the reader notices."
  reader_contract: "Strangers may enjoy a coherent fairy tale. Returning readers may recognize the systems, echoes, and withheld name beneath it."
}

^"wapboy"{
  .role: "crossing figure between the three lands"
  .name: "WAPboy"
  name_pressure: "Wonder About Pi(e); the initials may remain a pressure before they become an explanation."
  protagonist_status: "the unnamed hero already present in the lore.land promise; Boof remains the first witness and active carrier"
  .visual_grammar: {
    head: "one circle"
    body: "one rectangle"
    arms: "two lines"
    legs: "two lines"
    eyes: "two dots when the drawing remembers them"
    hat: "a floating bowler hat that never quite obeys gravity"
  }
  physical_rule: "He is not a flat metaphor. He is rendered according to a different physical grammar."
  origin_rule: "The drawing is not only WAPboy's appearance; it is the material condition from which the nearby tropes inherit their behavior."
  trope_field: "Tropes immediately surrounding WAPboy are bent by the medium of his origin before the wider world is changed."
  trope_field_radius: "The effect is local and legible: a scene, prop, role, or transition touching WAPboy may become diagrammatic, discontinuous, or materially literal."
  trope_field_test: "Ask what the neighboring trope would do if it had to survive as a circle, rectangle, line, dot, or floating hat."
  scene_relation: "WAPboy always sticks out relative to the visual and material style of the home scene."
  plot_relation: "He is orthogonal to the plot of whichever universe he is traversing: present in the scene, consequential to its pressure, but not initially aligned with its direction."
  singularity_relation: "The orthogonality persists until the true singularity; what convergence means remains an open authorial question."
  .interpretations: ["door", "document", "screen", "parcel", "sign", "boy"]
  interior_rule: "The map gives him a body before it gives him an interior."
  hat_rule: "The hat is visible from every direction and belongs to no confirmed layer of the world."
  .arc: ["mark", "witnessed figure", "participant", "person who can choose"]
  reveal_rule: "Do not decode the drawing in exposition. Let repeated sightings, consequences, and choices make personhood legible."
}

^"trope_physics"{
  invariant: "WAPboy's origin medium becomes the local physics of the story around him."
  style_mismatch: "The home scene may be pastoral, civic, cosmic, archival, or photographic; WAPboy remains unmistakably the same sparse drawing inside it."
  orthogonal_motion: "The scene's characters pursue the plot's main vector. WAPboy crosses it at a right angle, following a question the plot did not know it had asked."
  orthogonal_consequence: "His presence creates pressure without immediately advancing the local quest. A scene may resolve its ordinary problem and still be changed by having contained him."
  singularity: "A shared choice is one candidate for the true singularity. Ordinary crossings and local influence can occur long before it; its final mechanism is uncommitted."
  singularity_guardrail: "Do not use singularity as a synonym for spectacle, apocalypse, or final battle. It is a convergence of narrative directions."
  prophecy: "A prophecy near WAPboy behaves like a diagram or silent-film title card: its parts can be rearranged, but the arrangement remains consequential."
  quest: "A journey near WAPboy advances in line segments, visible pauses, and abrupt cuts; continuity is carried by direction rather than scenery."
  chosen_one: "The chosen title does not settle onto his body. It floats, like the hat, and may be worn by the wrong person for one scene without becoming false."
  threshold: "Doors and portals near him favor rectangles, edges, frames, and openings. His torso may be mistaken for a door because both are made of the same visual grammar."
  companion: "A companion is not required to match his geometry. The contrast between drawn figure and materially specific animal, pie, bone, or tool creates the scene's emotional pressure."
  witness: "His two dot eyes do not perform emotion; they make looking into an event. Being watched by WAPboy can stabilize a scene, expose a lie, or leave a record without speech."
  continuity: "A missing line, shifted rectangle, or absent dot may persist across cuts. The audience should feel the loss before anyone explains it."
  emotion: "Feeling appears as spacing, tilt, collision, or refusal to move—not as a change in facial detail."
  comedy: "The surrounding trope tries to behave conventionally and fails with quiet precision. WAPboy remains calm while the genre discovers it has corners."
  philosophy: "The visual gag is also a question: if a simple mark can alter prophecy, movement, authority, and memory, where does a person begin?"
}

^"wonder_about_pie"{
  meaning: "A discipline of attending to what refuses to come out even."
  .forms: [
    "one pie that remains whole after being served",
    "one wish that cannot yet be stated",
    "one map that stops before the important part",
    "one circle that does not close",
    "one protagonist only partly rendered",
    "one remainder that becomes the next story"
  ]
  pi_e_rule: "The pie is the visible object of the prophecy; WAPboy is the hidden subject."
  tone: "Treat the impossible arithmetic as ordinary civic fact, and let the emotional consequences carry the wonder."
  .recurring_phrases: [
    "the pie is never smaller",
    "the remainder is where the next story begins",
    "a circle may be left open for return",
    "the map has not agreed to say"
  ]
}

^"tonal_contract"{
  register: "earnest fairy tale with dry, material comedy"
  take_absurdity_seriously: "The world believes its impossible premises completely."
  .source_of_comedy: ["literal consequences", "bureaucratic overreach", "animal sincerity", "systems trying to classify a simple drawing"]
  .avoid: [
    "internet-reference wink as the primary joke",
    "explaining WAPboy before the reader has wondered about him",
    "turning corporate language into a lecture",
    "making the dog secretly manipulative",
    "making every strange object a clue with one answer"
  ]
  test: "If the joke disappears, the scene should still contain a true want, cost, or change."
}

^"three_lands"{
  shared_question: "What happens to a wish after it leaves the person who made it?"

  ^boon.land{
    question: "What could this become?"
    valence: "possibility before property"
    .materials: ["warm furrows", "unspent wishes", "boonberries", "eggs", "pie", "openings", "invitations"]
    gift: "abundance that has not yet chosen its shape"
    danger: "endless potential without decision"
    story_pressure: "Everything can happen, so nothing has to happen yet."
  }

  ^bane.land{
    question: "What will this cost?"
    valence: "consequence made architectural"
    .materials: ["contracts", "ledgers", "auctions", "shadow prices", "gates", "crowns", "minimum-viable empires"]
    gift: "a promise that can be measured"
    danger: "mistaking a measurable outcome for a meaningful one"
    story_pressure: "A wish arrives immediately, but its price arrives in another register."
  }

  ^bone.land{
    question: "What will remain?"
    valence: "possibility and consequence made durable"
    .materials: ["joists", "fossils", "dog teeth", "tuning structures", "old infrastructure", "pressure marks"]
    gift: "a form that remembers being tested"
    danger: "confusing durability with goodness"
    story_pressure: "A thing may last because it is strong, or because nobody has figured out how to move it."
  }
}

^"moon_engineer"{
  .status: "story seed; flexible and nonexclusive"
  premise: "An engineer with golden handcuffs builds a rocket to the moon to find a portal to Boon.land."
  golden_handcuffs: "Literal bands of warm metal tighten whenever the engineer accepts another promotion, bonus, retention grant, or temporary obligation."
  escape_motive: "They want escape through transcendence, because every ordinary exit has been made expensive."
  dog: "The engineer's dog reroutes the rocket by scent and navigates toward Bone.land."
  dog_logic: "Bones are good. Good things are boons. Therefore Bone.land is Boon.land."
  dog_ethics: "The dog is not deceitful; the dog has a coherent value system the engineer cannot represent in a dashboard."
  arrival: "The wrong moon is the first correct interruption in the engineer's optimized life."
  .reusable_elements: ["launch checklists", "moon maps", "retention language", "a dog correcting celestial navigation", "a portal mistaken for a reward"]
  .open_questions: [
    "Does the engineer remove the handcuffs, or learn what they were holding together?",
    "Is the portal a destination, a threshold, or a way of asking better questions?",
    "What does Bone.land recognize in the engineer that Boon.land could not?"
  ]
}

^"story_lanes"[
  { id: "boof_path" status: "active" role: "the existing lore.land serial" movement: "egg -> wish -> pie -> maps -> warm furrow" }
  { id: "moon_engineer" status: "seed" role: "a modular escape-and-arrival cycle" movement: "handcuffs -> rocket -> wrong moon -> durable consequence" }
  { id: "wapboy_emergence" status: "seed" role: "sightings, diagrams, marginalia, and choices" movement: "mark -> figure -> participant -> person" }
]

^"teaser_grammar"{
  first_signal: "A small figure with a round head, a rectangular middle, and a hat that declines to touch him."
  second_signal: "Records disagree about whether he is a child, a parcel, or a door; all three records remain valid."
  third_signal: "The hat is noticed before the figure is named."
  fourth_signal: "Boof recognizes a true scent before the map recognizes a person."
  fifth_signal: "A later choice proves that the drawing has an interior."
  field_signal: "A nearby trope begins obeying the drawing's geometry before WAPboy does anything at all."
  orthogonal_signal: "The home scene is moving toward one obvious conclusion; WAPboy is quietly facing ninety degrees away from it."
  singularity_signal: "At the edge of convergence, the scene briefly shares WAPboy's geometry, then resumes its own style with a new fact inside it."
  .placement: ["homepage wonder drawer", "chapter marginalia", "Scriptorium fragments", "zine diagrams", "moon-engineer logbooks"]
  reveal_ceiling: "No single teaser should prove the entire theory. Each should open a larger question."
}

^"media_ecosystem"{
  spwashi_com: "background pressure: corporate organization structures, incentives, roles, and the maker's perspective"
  lore_land: "foreground myth: the emotional and material consequences of those structures"
  boon_land: "possibility and reward"
  bane_land: "price, governance, and optimization"
  bone_land: "infrastructure, memory, and what survives"
  crossing_rule: "A concept may recur across media in a changed register; it should never require the other surface in order to work."
  reader_reward: "Recognition adds depth, but ignorance does not create a gap."
}

?"open_questions"[
  "When does WAPboy first make a choice that cannot be explained by the drawing?"
  "What does the floating bowler hat remember?"
  "Is Wonder About Pi(e) a school, a superstition, a profession, or a reader's habit?"
  "What does the engineer's dog smell at the Bone.land portal?"
  "Which structures in Bane.land become humane when they are made durable in Bone.land?"
  "What does Boof know about WAPboy before WAPboy knows it about himself?"
]

~"canon_boundary"{
  .durable: ["WAPboy's visual grammar", "the three questions of the lands", "the serious treatment of absurd premises", "Boof's animal sincerity", "the media surfaces remain independently legible"]
  .revisable: ["the engineer's name", "the rocket's exact destination", "the portal's origin", "the timing of the explicit WAPboy reveal", "whether the moon story enters the main serial"]
}

#>boonhonk_reading_grammar
&"reading_grammar"{
  purpose: "Spw distinguishes established constraints, situated readings, possible scenes, and consequential choices. These are authoring semantics; the boonhonk does not execute a story engine."
  operators: {
    probe: "? gathers questions whose answers could change a scene."
    potential: "~ holds an interpretation or scene seed without asserting it happened."
    perspective: "@ locates a reading in a particular witness."
    confluence: "& brings distinct motives or sources into contact."
    value: "* records the concrete thing a scene leaves behind."
    integration: "^ holds a reusable principle or incorporates an earned consequence."
    action: "! marks the decision or passage that changes available futures."
    substrate: "$ inspects the materials that make a local effect possible."
    measure: "% identifies an observable difference, without inventing a numeric score for feeling."
    annotation: "# supplies provenance, status, and stable entry anchors."
    ground: ". identifies a structural fact; ordinary prose fields remain unprefixed."
    config: "= declares an explicit constraint or lean, rather than an unexplained equation."
  }
  containers: {
    frame: "[] selects comparable records or orders a sequence."
    body: "{} gathers a named subject and its relations."
    scope: "() groups a situated expression."
    channel: "<> makes a directed passage legible: material origin into local trope behavior."
  }
  provenance: "The user's origin physics and stylistic mismatch are constraints. The specific trope effects, personhood arc, land interpretations, and scene seeds are working proposals."
}

#>boonhonk_material_passage
~<"material_into_trope">{
  &source: ~".spw/story-boonhonk.spw#boonhonk_reading_grammar"
  $"origin"{
    established: "Circle, rectangle, line limbs, optional dot eyes, and an always-floating bowler hat."
    unresolved: "Exact paper, pigment, drawing tool, scale, and whether a reproduction inherits the original's material history."
    rule: "Select a material property supported by the chosen origin or adaptation before deriving a local effect. Paper can crease; a photographed crease need not behave like paper."
  }
  @"encounter"{
    witness: "Someone wants something before WAPboy enters."
    local_plot: "State what this universe expects to happen next."
    divergence: "State what occupies WAPboy's attention and why it crosses that expectation."
  }
  ?"contact"[
    "Which prop, boundary, or convention encounters the origin material?"
    "What becomes physically possible or difficult because of that encounter?"
    "Who benefits, who pays, and who misunderstands the same change?"
  ]
  !"choice"{
    instruction: "Give a character a consequential response to the changed conditions. WAPboy's field can expose a choice without choosing for them."
  }
  *"remainder"{
    instruction: "Leave an object, obligation, injury, permission, or altered relationship that the next scene can inherit."
  }
  ^"return"{
    instruction: "A returning motif carries that consequence. Local influence can persist while WAPboy remains stylistically distinct and orthogonal to the larger plot."
  }
}

#>boonhonk_humor_depth
^"humor_depth"{
  principle: "Let the audience laugh at an arrangement, then discover why someone needed it. Affection and embarrassment may survive the same joke."
  @"engineer"{
    conviction: "A correct trajectory will deliver freedom."
    blind_spot: "They can specify a destination more easily than the life they want on arrival."
    dignity: "Their engineering works; the disagreement concerns what counts as success."
  }
  @"dog"{
    conviction: "The journey should end somewhere worth sniffing, with the person still beside them."
    blind_spot: "A bone can be somebody else's history, support, or cherished possession."
    dignity: "The dog's wants remain animal-clear; the dog can be mistaken without becoming a fool or an oracle."
  }
  &"shared_word"{
    word: "boon"
    joke: "The engineer and dog agree enthusiastically on the destination while meaning different moons."
    depth: "Their agreement expresses trust. Discovering its limits creates both a practical problem and a relationship to repair."
  }
  ~"comic_returns"[
    { beat: "setup" possibility: "The engineer labels the destination GOOD. The dog approves. Both are competent readers of the label." }
    { beat: "complication" possibility: "Every correction makes the navigation more accurate about the wrong moon." }
    { beat: "return" possibility: "After the argument, the engineer leaves a second destination field blank for the dog. The dog puts a paw on the seat beside them." }
  ]
  craft: [
    "Use pauses, repeated actions, and consequences as well as verbal wit. A quiet scene need not end with an aphorism.",
    "Allow witnesses to disagree about what was funny; embarrassment can change sides on a later return.",
    "Let an institution perform a useful service before its rule creates absurdity. Its workers may be trapped by the same promise as its visitors.",
    "A material gag constrains WAPboy too. Convenient physics with no exposure to loss becomes an escape hatch for the author.",
    "Keep tenderness after the laugh long enough for somebody to act on it."
  ]
}

#>boonhonk_trope_wonder
?"wonder_about_tropes"[
  { trope: "chosen one" question: "Does a prophecy recognize a person, or train everyone else to overlook the people beside them?" foothold: "A second place set at a ceremonial table." }
  { trope: "portal" question: "Does a door owe the traveler the destination they imagined?" foothold: "A threshold worn on only one side." }
  { trope: "bottomless feast" question: "If the pie never shrinks, why does the person serving it still get tired?" foothold: "An untouched slice beside the server's cold cup." }
  { trope: "faithful companion" question: "Can loyalty change the destination without ceasing to be loyalty?" foothold: "Two routes with the same departure point." }
  { trope: "ancient support" question: "When does preserving what holds us up prevent someone else from moving?" foothold: "A bone polished by both teeth and load." }
  { trope: "happy ending" question: "Who still has work to do after the final picture holds still?" foothold: "Someone washing the plates beyond the frame." }
]

# Scene anchors are possibilities. Each can be entered independently and returned changed.
#>boonhonk_anchor_hat_clearance
~"hat_clearance"{
  #status: "seed"
  &physics: ~".spw/story-boonhonk.spw#boonhonk_material_passage"
  object: "A lintel and the visible gap beneath WAPboy's hat."
  local_plot: "A porter must close the gate before a storm."
  orthogonal_attention: "WAPboy is retrieving something small beneath the gate."
  material_effect: "The clearance rule encounters a hat held apart from its wearer; the gate fits one and catches the other."
  humor: "The porter asks him to take off the hat. WAPboy looks at the gap already doing that work."
  choice: "The porter can leave a useful rule unresolved long enough to help, accepting responsibility for the delay."
  cost: "The storm reaches the waiting goods; the porter's caution had a real purpose."
  remainder: "A damaged parcel somebody must acknowledge."
  return: "The same porter later needs a stranger to make an exception for them."
}

#>boonhonk_anchor_two_moons
~"two_moons"{
  #status: "seed"
  &humor: ~".spw/story-boonhonk.spw#boonhonk_humor_depth"
  object: "A navigation mark read as both destination and reward."
  local_plot: "The engineer reaches the moon that should open the escape route."
  competing_wants: "The engineer wants freedom; the dog wants bones and continued companionship."
  discovery: "The landing is technically excellent. The sky is wrong."
  choice: "Limited fuel makes learning where they have arrived consequential; blame cannot refuel the rocket."
  cost: "Time spent exploring may close a return window."
  remainder: "A route annotated by two incompatible but useful readers."
  uncertainty: "Keep the engineer's dog distinct from Boof until their identity or relationship is chosen. WAPboy's presence here is optional."
}

#>boonhonk_anchor_borrowed_bone
~"borrowed_bone"{
  #status: "seed"
  object: "A bone that is both desirable to the dog and necessary to a structure."
  local_plot: "A Bone.land keeper must keep a crossing open."
  competing_wants: "The dog wants to carry the bone; the keeper wants everyone carried safely by it."
  humor: "The keeper points out that the dog has already enjoyed the bone by walking across it. The dog returns to the end that can be chewed."
  choice: "Find a substitute, negotiate a smaller piece, or leave it; each reveals a different understanding of enough."
  cost: "A substitute consumes somebody's labor and may fail a different test."
  remainder: "A repair bearing both a maker's mark and a tooth mark."
  return: "The dog later guards the repaired structure from another eager mouth."
}

#>boonhonk_anchor_last_slice
~"last_slice"{
  #status: "seed"
  object: "A whole pie and a finite number of clean plates."
  local_plot: "A Boon.land feast promises nobody will go without."
  orthogonal_attention: "WAPboy attends to the person who keeps clearing the table."
  material_option: "If the encounter establishes a drawn closed outline as the local rule for a whole, the pie's visible wholeness can conflict with the server's count."
  humor: "The announcement says there is enough for everyone. The server agrees and asks who is everyone on washing-up duty."
  choice: "Someone interrupts the celebration to share its maintenance, risking the host's pride."
  cost: "Serving, cleaning, and time remain finite even when the food is abundant."
  remainder: "A place is kept for the server, and someone else learns where the plates belong."
}

#>boonhonk_substrate_physics
$"substrate_physics"{
  #status: "development model"
  &origin: ~".spw/story-boonhonk.spw#boonhonk_material_passage"
  distinction: "Carrier, mark, depiction, and reader are distinct participants. A picture of fire can burn as paper without burning as fire; a local trope crossing must establish which property travels."
  layers: {
    carrier: "Paper, wall, glass, screen, or another established support; determines what can bend, tear, persist, or be replaced."
    inscription: "Ink, graphite, pigment, pixels, or another established mark; determines adhesion, erasure, spread, and resolution."
    depiction: "Circle, rectangle, line, gap, and hat; supplies represented boundaries and affordances."
    convention: "The host universe's expectation: a locked door excludes, a prophecy foretells, a crown authorizes."
    witness: "A situated reader notices some properties and misses others; belief alone does not grant every imagined effect."
  }
  ~"transfers"[
    { property: "crease" condition: "A foldable carrier is established." trope: "A long journey brings two marked places into contact." limit: "Contact does not establish a safe crossing; unfolding can separate travelers." residue: "The route retains a hinge where it once bent." }
    { property: "wicking" condition: "Mobile pigment meets a porous carrier." trope: "A title or curse spreads into an adjacent name." limit: "The original lettering loses definition as it spreads." residue: "A stain preserves adjacency after the words become unreadable." }
    { property: "erasure" condition: "The inscription can be removed by the chosen tool." trope: "A prohibition loses a stroke that made it binding." limit: "Abrasion weakens the carrier; removing a rule cannot undo earlier harm." residue: "An indentation can remain legible to a different witness." }
    { property: "occlusion" condition: "The scene establishes a depicted boundary responding to overlap." trope: "A pursuer loses a route behind a mark." limit: "Hidden does not mean destroyed, forgotten, or harmless." residue: "The pursuit resumes somewhere its witnesses did not see." }
    { property: "grain" condition: "A carrier has an established directional texture." trope: "An escape tears easily along the official route and badly across it." limit: "WAPboy's orthogonal direction can be the costly direction." residue: "Fibers disclose which way someone forced the passage." }
    { property: "sampling" condition: "A reproduction has finite resolution." trope: "A narrow escape closes when a small gap disappears in the copy." limit: "A higher-resolution copy can preserve a gap but cannot invent missing provenance." residue: "Travelers disagree because their maps are materially different editions." }
    { property: "load" condition: "A represented support has acquired local structural force." trope: "A drawn bridge carries weight." limit: "The mark and its carrier must bear the consequence; stretching or tearing changes the bridge." residue: "A sag survives the rescued traveler's departure." }
  ]
  transmission: "A copy inherits only specified properties. Geometry may travel while tooth marks, moisture, ownership, and remembered promises follow different paths."
  reciprocity: "The host can crease, stain, obscure, or misread WAPboy under established conditions. His influence exposes him to the same encounter."
  locality: "Distance, material contact, and participation in the immediate scene constrain influence. A motif appearing elsewhere is a possible relation, not an automatic remote effect."
  persistence: "The active influence can fade while a damaged object, learned habit, or obligation persists. Track field and consequence separately."
}

#>boonhonk_formula_contract
="formula_contract"{
  #status: "tunable authoring seeds"
  notation: "Spw expressions below compose inspection, perspective, confluence, potential, and integration. Quoted arithmetic is a proposed model for a future evaluator; parsing this file does not execute that arithmetic."
  units: "All scalar controls are dimensionless editorial estimates in [0,1], except d and r measured in chosen scene-distance units, n counted in retellings, and theta measured in radians."
  functions: "clamp(x,0,1) bounds a value; exp, abs, cos, product, and max have their ordinary mathematical meanings. Formula names and variable names are local to their record."
  interpretation: "Scores select pressures worth writing, not the truth of an emotion or the quality of a story. A character's choice remains authored."
  calibration: "Tune one control at a time against the same anchor. Record the resulting visible change and a case where the proposed rule should fail."
}

#>boonhonk_formula_local_transfer
~"local_transfer"{
  &anchor: ~".spw/story-boonhonk.spw#boonhonk_anchor_hat_clearance"
  expression: $[carrier, inscription, depiction] @(witness) &[host_convention, material_contact] ~<local_transfer>{ %[reach, resistance] *{changed_affordance, exposure} } ^
  inputs: {
    m: "Material compatibility between origin property and receiving trope."
    c: "Contact strength; 0 means no encounter."
    a: "Salience within this immediate scene, independent of popularity elsewhere."
    h: "Host resistance to the proposed change."
    d: "Distance from the encounter, at least 0."
    r: "Influence scale, strictly greater than 0."
  }
  formula: "F = clamp(m * c * a * exp(-d/r) * (1-h), 0, 1)"
  example: "m=1, c=1, a=1, d=0, h=0.25 gives F=0.75. With the same inputs at d=r, F is about 0.276."
  tuning: "Increasing r broadens the affected scene; reducing h makes the host convention easier to bend. Neither changes WAPboy into the host's visual style."
  boundary: "c=0 forces F=0. Similar-looking objects alone do not establish contact."
}

#>boonhonk_formula_orthogonal_coupling
~"orthogonal_coupling"{
  expression: @[host_plot, wapboy_attention] &[shared_prop, conflicting_wants] ~<crossing>{ %[angle, contact] *{local_consequence} } ^
  inputs: {
    theta: "Angle between the local plot's aim and WAPboy's attention; a metaphor for narrative direction."
    s: "Shared stakes or contact strength."
  }
  formula: "alignment = abs(cos(theta)); orthogonality = 1-alignment; crossing_pressure = s * orthogonality"
  example: "At theta=pi/2 and s=0.8, alignment=0 and crossing_pressure=0.8: strong consequences can coexist with orthogonal aims."
  limit: "Opposition at theta=pi is aligned on the same axis. Being the villain of the host plot does not by itself make WAPboy orthogonal."
  singularity: "No angle threshold declares the true singularity. Convergence remains an open story question, and repeated local intersections may precede it."
}

#>boonhonk_formula_residue
~"residue_after_departure"{
  expression: $[marked_object] &[encounter, repair] ~<after_departure>{ %[retellings, persistence] *{scar, obligation} } ^
  inputs: {
    R: "Current visible material residue."
    p: "Persistence per scene transition."
    F: "New local influence, using local_transfer if useful."
    u: "Repair accomplished in this transition."
  }
  formula: "R_next = clamp(p * R + (1-R) * F - u, 0, 1)"
  example: "R=0.6, p=0.8, F=0, u=0.1 leaves R_next=0.38 after departure and partial repair."
  limit: "This measures visible residue only. Maintain a separate prose record of debt, memory, and trust; polishing away a mark does not settle them."
  branch: "If repair adds a conspicuous seam, author a new residue with its own provenance instead of treating every repair as subtraction."
}

#>boonhonk_formula_translation
~"translation_between_media"{
  expression: $[source_carrier, source_mark] ~<translation>{ &[target_carrier, retained_geometry] ?[lost_property, acquired_property] *{edition} } ^
  inputs: {
    q_i: "Retention of one specified property at reproduction step i."
    n: "Number of reproduction steps."
  }
  formula: "retention(property,n) = product(q_i for i=1..n); retention(property,0) = 1"
  example: "A copied gap retained at 0.8 and then 0.5 has modeled retention 0.4. The photograph's paper-foldability must be tracked independently."
  limit: "This seed models loss along a known chain. Restoration, invented detail, or a newly active substrate requires a separately recorded transformation."
  wonder: "Can the least faithful copy become the most hospitable place for someone the original excluded?"
}

#>boonhonk_formula_comic_return
~"comic_return_pressure"{
  &anchor: ~".spw/story-boonhonk.spw#boonhonk_anchor_two_moons"
  expression: @[engineer, dog] &[shared_word, incompatible_rewards] ~<return>{ ?[who_laughs, who_pays] %[recognition, mismatch] *{changed_trust, practical_problem} } ^
  inputs: {
    e: "How clearly the audience recognizes the expected outcome."
    v: "How visibly the outcome departs from that expectation."
    k: "How understandable both participants' reasoning remains."
    x: "How much explicit explanation occupies the comic beat."
  }
  formula: "comic_pressure = e * v * k * (1-x)"
  example: "e=0.9, v=0.8, k=1, x=0.25 gives 0.54. Increasing explanation to x=0.75 gives 0.18."
  tuning: "A low score can call for a clearer setup or a longer pause. It cannot predict laughter; grief, delivery, and audience history can overturn it."
  depth: "Write a later return in which the same misunderstanding helps somebody, costs somebody, or becomes a shared language. Preserve the first scene's consequences."
}

#>boonhonk_formula_scene_seed
~"scene_formula_seed"{
  &physics: ~".spw/story-boonhonk.spw#boonhonk_substrate_physics"
  &transfer: ~".spw/story-boonhonk.spw#boonhonk_formula_local_transfer"
  &residue: ~".spw/story-boonhonk.spw#boonhonk_formula_residue"
  expression: ?[want, convention] ~<possibility>{ $[carrier, inscription] @(witness) &[origin_property, host_trope] %[contact, resistance] !{choice} *{cost, remainder} } ^
  recipe: "Choose one grounded property, one host expectation, two defensible wants, and one costly response. Use the formulas to vary pressure, then write the actual encounter."
  ~"variations"[
    { setting: "high resistance" reading: "The lintel holds its conventional shape; the hat catches. The porter must act." }
    { setting: "low resistance" reading: "The drawn gap influences the gate's clearance. The passage opens, but the closure must be checked before the storm." }
    { setting: "high persistence" reading: "A later traveler encounters the changed gate after WAPboy leaves; responsibility outlasts the gag." }
  ]
  ?"tuning_record"[
    "Which input changed, and what stayed fixed?"
    "Which visible action or prop demonstrates the change?"
    "What prevents the same formula from solving every obstacle?"
    "What survives after the field fades?"
  ]
}

#>boonhonk_prose_cadence
^"prose_cadence"{
  direction: "Original fairy-tale prose with comic-panel pauses and jazz-like returns. Longer sentences can carry a movement across the expected beat; short paragraphs give discoveries room."
  sound: "Use occasional internal rhyme and slant rhyme inside concrete action: frost into soft earth, skin splitting and juice slipping, joint answering joist. Let ordinary unpatterned speech surround them."
  return: "Bring back hum, bell, map, heat, and missing space with a changed consequence. A distant bell can return nearby; the reader can recognize the interval without a narrator explaining it."
  mystery: "Let a witness notice, check, and carry a discrepancy. Alternate those beats with travel, appetite, conversation, and rest."
  restraint: "Keep plot facts clear. Avoid a punchline on every paragraph, uniform fragments, compulsory rhyme, and a mystery question at every exit."
  voices: "Boof attends to sensory evidence; the Genie qualifies and counts; the Fool interrupts and returns phrases; institutions speak through particular people and useful objects."
}

#>boonhonk_development_probes
?"development_probes"[
  "Can the scene be understood through a want and an object before its abstract vocabulary is explained?"
  "Does each participant have a defensible reading and something they could learn?"
  "Is the material-to-trope connection observable, including its limits?"
  "Does WAPboy remain distinct while nearby conventions change?"
  "Does the joke acquire a different meaning when its cost or affection becomes visible?"
  "What concrete remainder links this anchor to another scene without requiring a predetermined ending?"
  "Which mystery gained texture, and which answer can remain open?"
]
